## 1. SQL vs NoSQL — Key Differences
So basically, both SQL and NoSQL are types of databases but they work in completely different ways.
SQL stores data in tables, just like an Excel sheet — rows and columns. The schema is fixed, meaning you have to define the structure before inserting any data. Examples are MySQL and PostgreSQL.
NoSQL stores data in a flexible format — could be documents, key-value pairs, or graphs. There is no fixed schema, so you can add fields whenever you want. Examples are MongoDB, Redis, Cassandra.
A simple example to understand —
SQL looks like this :
sqlCREATE TABLE students (
  id INT PRIMARY KEY,
  name VARCHAR(100),
  gpa FLOAT
);
MongoDB (NoSQL) looks like this —
js{
  "name": "Rahul",
  "gpa": 3.8,
  "hobbies": ["cricket", "coding"]   // extra fields allowed, no restrictions
}
The main difference is — SQL is rigid and structured, NoSQL is flexible. SQL scales vertically (buy a better machine), NoSQL scales horizontally (add more machines).

## 2. CAP Theorem
CAP theorem was given by Eric Brewer. It says that any distributed system cannot guarantee all three of these properties at the same time —

C — Consistency → every read gets the latest data
A — Availability → system always gives a response
P — Partition Tolerance → system keeps running even if network fails between nodes

Why can't we have all three together?
Because in real life, network failures are bound to happen — packets drop, nodes crash. So P has to be kept always, otherwise there is no point of a distributed system.
Now when a partition happens, the system has only two choices —

Either give correct data (keep Consistency) — means some requests get rejected → Availability is lost


Or respond to everyone (keep Availability) — means stale/old data might be returned → Consistency is lost

So at any point, only two properties can be guaranteed —

MongoDB → CP
Cassandra → AP
Traditional RDBMS on single node → CA


## 3. Three Scenarios Where MongoDB is Better
Scenario 1 — When you need a Flexible Schema
Say there is an e-commerce site selling shoes, laptops, and food — all three have completely different attributes. In SQL you would need lots of NULL columns or very complex table structures. In MongoDB each product can just be its own document —
js{ "type": "shoe", "size": 9, "color": "black" }
{ "type": "laptop", "ram": "16GB", "brand": "Dell" }
No problem at all.
Scenario 2 — When data is Nested or Hierarchical
Like a blog post that has comments, and comments have likes. In SQL you would need 3-4 tables and multiple JOINs. In MongoDB everything sits inside one document —
js{
  "post": "Great day today",
  "comments": [
    { "user": "Amit", "text": "Nice one!" }
  ]
}
One single read and you get everything.
Scenario 3 — When you need High Write Speed (IoT or Real-time apps)
If thousands of sensors are sending data every second, MongoDB's horizontal sharding handles it easily. A relational database would struggle to manage that kind of load efficiently.

## 4. Why Does MongoDB Use BSON Instead of JSON?
JSON looks nice and readable for humans, but it is not efficient for storing data internally. That is why MongoDB uses BSON, which stands for Binary JSON.
Why is BSON better?
First — more data types. JSON only has string, number, boolean, array, object, null. BSON adds Date, ObjectId, int32, int64, Decimal128 — all of which are very important for a database to function properly.
Second — speed. BSON is in binary format so the machine reads and writes it much faster. JSON is plain text and has to be parsed character by character, which is slow.
Third — ObjectId. MongoDB gives every document a unique _id which is a BSON ObjectId — 12 bytes, auto-generated, and it even stores the timestamp inside it.
Quick comparison —
JSON :  { "age": 25 }  →  stored as text, slow to parse
BSON :  age = \x19     →  stored as binary, direct memory access
Simply put — JSON is for humans to read, BSON is for machines to process.

## 5. MongoDB Queries — Students with GPA > 3.5 and Enrolled in CS101
Assume the collection name is students and documents look like this —
js{
  "name": "Priya",
  "gpa": 3.9,
  "courses": ["CS101", "MATH101"]
}


Basic query —

jsdb.students.find({
  gpa: { $gt: 3.5 },
  courses: "CS101"
})


Show only name and GPA (projection) —

jsdb.students.find(
  { gpa: { $gt: 3.5 }, courses: "CS101" },
  { name: 1, gpa: 1, _id: 0 }
)


Sort by GPA in descending order —

jsdb.students.find({
  gpa: { $gt: 3.5 },
  courses: "CS101"
}).sort({ gpa: -1 })
Count how many students match —
jsdb.students.countDocuments({
  gpa: { $gt: 3.5 },
  courses: "CS101"
})


Aggregation — also find the average GPA —

jsdb.students.aggregate([
  {
    $match: {
      gpa: { $gt: 3.5 },
      courses: "CS101"
    }
  },
  {
    $group: {
      _id: null,
      totalStudents: { $sum: 1 },
      averageGPA: { $avg: "$gpa" }
    }
  }
])
